(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608760000-jvm-free-multi-route-http-service-host")
  (:adr/title "amu: a JVM-free, multi-route HTTP host for compiled Kotoba guests (kotoba/http-service)")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/body
  "## Context\n\nThe owner asked to remove the JVM dependency from `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app`\n(a full JVM Clojure webapp) and run it via the `kotoba` CLI, authorizing new\nlibraries in `kotoba-lang` where needed.\n\nWhat existed before this ADR:\n\n- `kotoba-lang/amu`'s `runtime/wasi-service.mjs` — a real JVM-free HTTP host,\n  but extremely narrow: one route (`POST /v1/run`), a request schema locked to\n  `{entry, args, format}` with `args` bounded to five canonical i64 decimal\n  strings. No arbitrary body, no route table, no sessions.\n- `kotoba-lang/mesh` — the closest prior art for the DESIGN PATTERN, not a\n  solution: its ahead-of-time-compiled `route.kotoba` decision core is real and\n  landed (ADR-2608120200), but its host runs on JVM + Chicory. Reusing its\n  core verbatim was considered and rejected: it hard-codes murakumo's fixed\n  `/mesh/http/<route>` prefix scheme.\n- `kotoba-lang/kotobase-server`'s README records that this workspace's own\n  flagship production server has NOT achieved a JVM/Rust-free replacement\n  either (ADR-2607072000, pinned Rust binary, \"no cljc successor\"). The gap is\n  real; closing it here does not make kotobase.net or cloud-itonami-app\n  JVM-free.\n\nPer CLAUDE.md's runtime priority (`kotoba wasm runtime > clojurewasm >\nClojureScript > nbb`, JVM/bb last resort), a Node-hosted `:js-kotoba-v1`\nrestricted-ESM module (`amu compile --target js`) is a sanctioned way to run\ncompiled Kotoba guests without a JVM **at runtime**. The compiler still needs a\nJDK to produce the artifact — a build-time tool dependency, explicitly outside\nADR-2607198300's \"no JVM at runtime\" rule, the same way gcc must run somewhere\nto build a native binary.\n\nMeasured while building: `--target js` is a BETTER fit for an HTTP host than\nthe WASI/wasm32 path `wasi-service.mjs` uses. A restricted-ESM instance passes\nplain JS values directly (strings as strings, i64 as BigInt, keywords as\n`\":kw\"`, bool as bool) and is fuel-metered per `instantiateKotoba()`, so a\nguest call either returns or traps — it cannot spin the event loop. No\n`worker_threads` isolation is needed.\n\n## Decision\n\n**`runtime/http-service.mjs` in `kotoba-lang/amu`** — a multi-route, JSON-body,\nJVM-free HTTP host for `:js-kotoba-v1` guests, following the decision/mechanism\nsplit `mesh` established, ported rather than copied:\n\n- `runtime/http/route-decide.kotoba`, compiled ahead of time and called on\n  every request, decides built-in kind, outcome, and HTTP status. It is handed\n  a host-supplied BOOLEAN for \"is this route bound\" — never the route table\n  (mirrors mesh's ADR-2608112100 boundary: a core handed one decision's worth\n  of scalars delegates; a core handed a growing collection does not).\n- The host owns the route table, socket/JSON mechanics, sha256 seal\n  verification of every guest module, a hard refusal of any module declaring\n  non-empty `requiredCapabilities` (this host grants zero capabilities — every\n  handler and the core itself are `kotoba/pure`), and bounded\n  concurrency/body size (65536 bytes, the measured `stringLimits.valueBytes`\n  ceiling on this target).\n- Bodies cross as `:string`, not `:document`: the host validates that request\n  and response are each syntactically valid JSON and passes the exact bytes —\n  no restructuring.\n\n**`scripts/cloud-itonami-route-parity.cljs`** — one parameterised harness\nproving admission-decision parity for `cloud-itonami-app`'s existing Kotoba\nroute cores. It replaced three copies of itself. What is shared is the\nmechanism (compile, run both runtimes, compare, floors, mutation hook, exit\ncodes); what is deliberately NOT shared is the claim — each route's battery\nstays per-route data, because folding six batteries into one generated list\nwould quietly change what the landed proofs asserted.\n\nLanded on `kotoba-lang/amu` main: `391124d` (host, PR #680), `8a5152f`\n(PR #683), `e4a1ef2`, `710e20d` (harness + four routes, PR #691).\n\n## What has been proven\n\n**The host works, end to end, without a JVM.** `scripts/http-service-e2e.cljs`\nrecompiles the decision core fresh and fails on drift, then starts a real\n`node:http` server and fires real `fetch` requests — 18 assertions. It covers\ndispatch (`GET /health` 200, `POST /echo` round-tripping arbitrary JSON\nbyte-for-byte, unregistered route 404, wrong method 404, malformed body 400,\noversized body 413), startup refusal of a digest mismatch, and startup refusal\nof a module that requires a capability. Its **delegation** test is the one that\nseparates \"calls the compiled core\" from \"has its own hardcoded `cond`\":\nswapping in a decision-core double with different status mapping changes an\nunbound route from 404 to 410. Separately, real `curl` requests were run\nagainst a standalone `node runtime/http-service.mjs` process, with `ps`\nconfirming no `java` process running while it served.\n\n**Six of `cloud-itonami-app`'s route cores give identical admission answers\nunder two independent runtimes** — the JVM/KIR-interpreter oracle path the\nproduction server actually takes, and `:js-kotoba-v1` on this host:\n\n| core | export | cases | mismatches |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `health_core` | `health-route?` | 21 | 0 |\n| `oauth_resource_core` | `oauth-resource-route?` | 28 | 0 |\n| `did_web_core` | `did-web-route?` | 34 | 0 |\n| `did_web_core` | `did-log-route?` | 34 | 0 |\n| `did_web_core` | `did-witness-route?` | 34 | 0 |\n| `domain_binding_core` | `nonce-route?` | 34 | 0 |\n\n`cloud-itonami-app` is never modified by the harness; its sources are read\nbyte-for-byte and its own shipped namespaces are executed via `clojure -M`.\n\nThree floors run on every route, because a parity check that cannot fail\nproves nothing:\n\n1. **positive-admitted-by-both**, checked separately from mismatch counting —\n   a battery on which both runtimes answer `false` everywhere \"agrees\" while\n   proving nothing;\n2. **cross-admission coverage**, computed from the registry — every route's\n   battery must include the other `.well-known` routes as negatives. This\n   matters because these cores are siblings by design, and it is the only way\n   the adversarial pair `did.json` / `did.jsonl` (one a strict prefix of the\n   other) gets tested at all;\n3. **mutation verification**, run rather than assumed. Injecting a single\n   wrongly-admitted negative produced\n   `FAIL -- 1/34 mismatches: [\"GET\" \"/.well-known/did.json/\"] js=true jvm=false`\n   at exit 1, with the positive case undisturbed — so the failure came from the\n   mismatch path, and what was broken is what was reported.\n\n**A host/core asymmetry on `did-witness.json`, recorded so the case row is not\nmisread**: `server.clj`'s wrapper admits `POST` and normalises it to `\"GET\"`\nbefore consulting the core, so the core answers `false` to POST while the\nendpoint accepts it. Correct layering, not a parity defect.\n\n## The migration unit — a correction\n\nThis ADR initially treated \"another decision core proven\" as its unit of\nprogress, and planned a seventh. **That framing is withdrawn** (2026-08-29, on\nthe owner's challenge 「kotoba は判定のみ, みたいな refactor はもう古いのでは?」).\n\nADR-2608261100 (accepted 2026-08-26) demotes decision-core extraction from the\nmigration unit to a **fallback**, licensed only when a *named* backend cannot\nadmit the value, and names this ADR's own target: 「wasm / web /\n`:js-kotoba-v1` に対して、最初から判断核へ潰さない。そこでは application を\napplication として書く。」 `:js-kotoba-v1` admits strings, maps, records,\n`:document` and `cond` — `amu`'s `examples/todo-app.kotoba` is a whole\napplication on it. The word-typed ceiling that licenses a decision core is\n`native`'s, not this backend's, so the fallback's precondition was never met\nhere.\n\nWhat is **not** withdrawn is the measured property above: six cores agreeing\nacross runtimes is a real result about a runtime swap, and its harness stays.\nWhat is withdrawn is reading that as an application moving.\n\nIn fairness to the record: those six cores were not created here. They already\nexisted in `cloud-itonami-app` under its own ADR-0038/0039/0040, written when\nthe JVM owned the socket. Adopting them as a first probe was reasonable;\nplanning a seventh was not.\n\n(Earlier revisions of this document stated the superseded framing and stacked\nfour same-day addenda. Per ADR-2607257000 a document states 最新状態のみ and\n`git log -p` holds the history; this body was consolidated on 2026-08-29 so the\ncurrent position reads in one pass.)\n\n## What is real and what is representative\n\nTwo routes are also SERVED on this host as demonstrations. Their JSON shapes\nare read from `server.clj`, and every field that a bare demo process cannot\nhonestly produce says so literally rather than carrying an invented value:\n\n- `GET /health` — `service` and `schema` are real fixed constants copied\n  verbatim; `store` and `drive-store` are a live deployment's fingerprint and\n  selected backend, so they read `representative-…`.\n- `GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp` — `resource_name`,\n  `bearer_methods_supported` and `scopes_supported` are real constants from\n  `oauth-resource/metadata*`; `resource` and `authorization_servers` are\n  configuration-derived and read `representative-…`. **That gap is wider than\n  `/health`'s**: `metadata*` throws `:oauth-resource/no-authorization-server`\n  when none is configured, so a real deployment in this host's state would 500\n  rather than serve a degraded document.\n\n**The four did:web / domain-binding routes are proved for ADMISSION ONLY and\nare deliberately not served.** Grounded in `server.clj`: `did.json` is resolved\nper-`Host` and 404s when no domain is published, carrying real verification\nkeys and `alsoKnownAs`; `did.jsonl` is a signed did:webvh log;\n`did-witness.json` is witness signatures; the domain-binding route serves a\nper-binding secret nonce. Serving demo versions would mean fabricating\ndocuments that look real.\n\n## Honest scope — how far this is from \"cloud-itonami-app runs without a JVM\"\n\n**Not close.** Six of roughly thirty routes, admission decisions only, and four\nof those six are not served JVM-free at all. No application logic has moved.\n\nEach of the following is untouched and needs its own substantial work:\n\n- **WebAuthn ceremonies** — not investigated; likely needs COSE/CBOR\n  attestation verification callable from Kotoba, which no target has today.\n- **OAuth redirects / session cookies** — this host has zero cookie or session\n  primitives; sessions need a capability-gated store, not a pure guest.\n- **Mail sync / drive sync** — the atomic outbound authorities exist\n  (`capability-http-fetch`, `capability-http-post`) but no guest-callable\n  client library is compiled to `.kotoba`.\n- **LLM tool-calling loop** — no `.kotoba` LLM client exists.\n- **Wallet / payments** — out of scope for any agent per this workspace's\n  safety floor.\n- **Host→tenant resolution** — which the four unserved documents all need.\n\n`cloud-itonami-app` has not been modified by any slice of this work, and its\nrunning server was not touched.\n\n## Next slice\n\nA `kotoba/app` vertical slice on `:js-kotoba-v1`, written as an application\nrather than collapsed to a predicate: `state + event -> next-state`, with\n`todo-app.kotoba`'s `init` / `view` / `step` as the shape, the guest holding\nproduct semantics as ordinary values, and the host holding only mechanism.\nADR-2607279200 decision 5's four-way classification is the default; core\nextraction is not.\n\nIts concrete blocker is not a language limit. This host passes a request body\nacross as a validated-syntactically-JSON `:string` with no structural parsing\ninside the guest — and an application that cannot read its own request body has\nnothing to be an application about. `kotoba-lang/json` gained a `.kotoba`\nscalar field extractor on 2026-08-29 (ADR-2608292330, `:effects #{}`, using\nonly string operations qualified on every backend including native), which\ncloses the capability half. The remaining half is **fuel**:\n`instantiateKotoba` opens a fixed, never-replenished budget, default 512 units\ncharged per function entry, which caps traversal at a few hundred bytes against\nthis host's 65536-byte body ceiling. Raising it deliberately at compile time\n(`--fuel`), justified against the body bound admitted, and keeping the sha256\npin, is what the next slice must settle.\n\nDo not add a seventh `[:string :string] -> :bool` route.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `amu` gains a second, general-purpose HTTP host alongside the narrow\n  `wasi-service.mjs`. Both remain: different compile targets and different\n  tradeoffs — wasi-service.mjs's Worker isolation is still preferable for a\n  guest needing a hard OS-level cutoff rather than a fuel budget.\n- No new capability-authority repo was created. `capability-http-serve` /\n  `capability-net-listen` were considered against\n  `manifest/repository-rules.edn`'s `capability-` prefix declaration, which\n  requires `:execution :none` — a contract-only repo — and this host is\n  execution, not a contract. The trigger to add one would be a slice where\n  GUESTS, not the host, need listen/accept authority; today's guests are\n  capability-free pure functions.\n- The parity harness is reusable but should not be fed more string-equality\n  routes. Its remaining value is as a template for proving a *harder* property\n  across runtimes, not for extending a list.\n")))
